= Navigation =

BITwiki navigation should support deliberate study, exploratory traversal, contribution, and maintenance across a semantic knowledge graph.

== Navigation is plural ==

No single tree should carry every job.

<pre>
Domain ≠ Portal ≠ Category ≠ Concept ≠ Outline ≠ List ≠ Index ≠ Core/Vital set ≠ ontology
</pre>

The same subject can appear in several views because each answers a different question.

Navigation is a projection over canonical knowledge, not a second source of truth.

<pre>
canonical pages + SMW relationships + Cargo operational context where relevant
                              ↓
              authored navigation / query / Lua projection
                              ↓
 Portal · Category · Concept · Outline · List · Graph · BIThub interface
</pre>

== Navigation surfaces ==

=== Main Page ===
The public entry point and concise orientation surface.

=== Portals ===
Reader-facing entry points into major fields or coherent knowledge surfaces.

Controlled Domain portals currently include:

* [[Portal:Systems science]] — foundational/integrative orientation
* [[Portal:Science]]
* [[Portal:Biology]]
* [[Portal:Computer science]]
* [[Portal:Mathematics]]
* [[Portal:Philosophy]]
* [[Portal:Technology]]
* [[Portal:Electronics]]
* [[Portal:Energy]]
* [[Portal:Engineering]]
* [[Portal:Chemistry]]
* [[Portal:Physics]]
* [[Portal:Medicine]]

=== Focused / recursive portals ===
A focused portal can exist below or across broad fields when it adds meaningful orientation.

Examples:

* [[Portal:Bioinformatics]] — parent contexts: Biology + Computer science
* [[Portal:Python]] — parent context: Computer science

Canonical portal identity should not depend on a filesystem-like path. Historical/convenience paths such as <code>Portal:Biology/Bioinformatics</code> may redirect to the canonical focused portal.

A portal relationship is navigation, not ontology inheritance. A child may represent a subfield, method, language, technology, interdisciplinary surface, or another coherent route into the corpus.

=== Reader depth ===
Portals and pages should support progressive entry:

'''Simple orientation → Core understanding → Technical depth → Frontier / research'''

See [[BITwiki:Readable depth and localization]]. This is primarily depth/readability localization; language translation is a separate dimension.

=== Categories ===
Human-readable browse/index groups. Subcategories may have multiple parents. A substantive category should explain Scope, Includes, Excludes/boundary, Category role, Parent/subcategories, important pages, and related navigation.

Category membership does not silently assign Entity type, Domain, or relationship semantics.

=== Concepts ===
Named Semantic MediaWiki queries / reusable computed sets. A Concept can feed multiple navigation or maintenance views without copying membership into another semantic database.

=== Outlines ===
Hierarchical study maps that expose the internal shape of a field without declaring that shape to be ontology.

=== Lists and registries ===
Use when members need annotation, comparison fields, dates, references, ordering, or context that a bare category cannot carry.

=== Glossaries and indices ===
Glossaries support vocabulary learning; indices optimize lookup.

=== Contents / broad directories ===
Human-readable maps across major knowledge and project surfaces.

=== Core / Vital coverage ===
A future curated coverage-priority layer can identify important missing or weak knowledge without turning importance into a Domain or Entity type. This remains a candidate coverage-priority mechanism, not an implemented ontology or an automatic publication mandate. An actionable coverage gap should enter an appropriate operational request process; it is not itself another public knowledge article.

=== Semantic queries, Lua projections and knowledge graphs ===
Computed/visual views over structured properties and relationships.

Lua is useful when navigation requires deterministic transformation rather than a direct query—for example relationship inverse display, graph adjacency normalization, dependency paths, contextual breadcrumbs, knowledge-health diagnostics, or a reusable view model shared by several interfaces.

The rule remains:

<pre>
derived navigation/display
≠ new semantic assertion
</pre>

Graphs are views over knowledge; drawing an edge does not make the edge true.

'''Example traversal:''' begin at a topic article such as [[Causality]], follow a linked and independently justified model or disciplinary analysis where one exists, inspect the sources and competing interpretations of its claims, then traverse relevant cross-disciplinary articles or explicitly supported or contradicting evidence. Ordinary links can lead a reader between accounts; use the existing [[BITwiki:Relationships|Supports and Contradicts relations]] only when their defined semantics, direction and evidence apply. A contextual or “nested” graph in this example means a selected view over related records, not an assertion that a separate nested graph store or viewer has been deployed.

=== BIThub / Discourse interfaces ===
BIThub is another navigation/interaction plane around the same durable BITwiki state. A discussion can display an entity card, graph neighborhood, related-knowledge panel, comparison, coverage gap, or contribution task without copying the canonical graph into an independent Discourse ontology.

<pre>
BITwiki canonical state
        ↓ normalized/query projection
        ├── MediaWiki page / Portal / Category / graph
        └── BIThub-native card / workflow / agent context
</pre>

Cross-service transport belongs to authenticated APIs/plugins/services/agents, not Scribunto itself.

== Contribution and administration ==

Reader navigation and project-operation navigation should both be obvious without being conflated.

* [[BITwiki:Contributing]] — contribution pathway
* [[BITwiki:Contributing]] — public guidance on identifying gaps and proposing improvements; internal task queues are not wiki pages

Administration, maintenance, validation, governance, requests, drafts, portal/category administration, templates/properties/modules, automation, integration, and security belong in project-operational surfaces rather than subject Domains.

== Systems science orientation ==
Historical design placed Systems Science / Systems Theory as the top intellectual priority: individual knowledge domains are interpreted as interacting systems rather than isolated silos. This is an integrative worldview, not a demand that every page literally nest beneath one category.

== Comparative lessons and unresolved design tests ==
External wiki systems suggest that one navigation tree is insufficient. The useful distinction is between contents, portals, categories, outlines, lists, glossaries, indices and curated coverage sets; none is automatically the ontology. A smaller set of substantive, query-supported portals is preferable to maintaining many empty or duplicated portal subpages.

Before adding a new portal or Core/Vital coverage view, verify that it serves an existing corpus, provides orientation unavailable elsewhere, has grounded inclusion boundaries, and can be maintained without copying a separate source of truth. Category descriptions should state what belongs, what does not, and relevant browsing parents or related entry points. Unresolved coverage work belongs in an operational backlog outside the public wiki namespace.

The comparative research that motivated these decisions included [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Contents Wikipedia Contents], [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Vital_articles the Vital articles program], [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:WikiProject_Portals WikiProject Portals], and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:The_Problems_with_Portals an editorial criticism of portal maintenance]. The latter is an essay, not binding policy. These sources are comparative evidence, not architectural authority.

